{"summary":"Fix literal substring delimiter matching and consumption","explanation":"`LiteralPolicy::Find` currently uses `find_first_of`, so a multi-character delimiter matches any one of its bytes; `Length` then consumes only one byte. In `Split.APIExamples`, the test expects `v` to contain `"a", "b", "c"` but observes `"a", "", "b", "", "c"`. In `Split.UTF8`, `v` is expected to contain `"a", "b"` but instead contains 14 elements, including empty strings. In `Split.SubstrDelimiter`, the `results` assertions expect 2, 1, or 3 elements across seven cases, but observe 3, 2, or 5 elements; the listed actual values show extra empty fields or pieces split inside the delimiter. Searching for the complete literal substring and consuming its full length restores those boundaries. The single-character `ByString` fast path remains unchanged, and empty-delimiter behavior remains handled by `GenericFind`’s special case.","edits":[{"path":"absl/strings/str_split.cc","old":"struct LiteralPolicy {
  static size_t Find(absl::string_view text, absl::string_view delimiter,
                     size_t pos) {
    return text.find_first_of(delimiter, pos);
  }
  static size_t Length(absl::string_view delimiter) {
    return delimiter.empty() ? 0 : 1;
  }
};","new":"struct LiteralPolicy {
  static size_t Find(absl::string_view text, absl::string_view delimiter,
                     size_t pos) {
    return text.find(delimiter, pos);
  }
  static size_t Length(absl::string_view delimiter) {
    return delimiter.length();
  }
};"}]}
